[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cleanly. So underneath all this, the setup is actually pretty simple. On one side, we\'ve got the voice agent. Okay, great. That handles conversation, interruptions, status, cancelling, a lot of the fast stuff. On the other side,'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clogged code is connected over ACP. That\'s the side touching files, running tools, and doing the slow work. Now, mechanically, it\'s really two lanes here. And once you see that, this design'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is a bit more obvious. There\'s a lightweight front-end voice agent whose only job is to hold the conversation. It answers the easy questions immediately. What\'s the status? Cancel that. Never mind. Then there is your real coding'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent on the back end and they talk over the agent client protocol, the ACP. Real work gets handed over as an async task and runs on its own. When it finishes, the result gets injected back into the'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'live conversation. So the voice layer never blocks on the slow thing. And because it\'s a protocol rather than an integration, the backend is swappable. Clawed Code, Codeex, Open Code, and a'

